# 5. AgentLens — this is eerily close to your TEE rental concept

AgentLens describes a trust-first marketplace where people compare evidence, rent an agent and execute it with platform-provided capabilities.

Most importantly:

> renting gives **time-limited execution access**, not source-code ownership.

([GitHub][4])

That's exactly the distinction we made:

```text
BUY WORKER
≠
LEASE CAPABILITY
```

Their public repo apparently includes contracts, integration schemas, sandbox and audit-facing infrastructure while keeping some production runtime/routing/billing private. ([GitHub][4])

The abstraction to steal is:

```text
CapabilityLease
{
    worker
    renter
    valid_from
    valid_until

    allowed_capabilities[]
    max_invocations
    max_cost

    data_policy
    execution_boundary

    outputs_allowed[]
}
```

Then plug Phala/dstack attestation into Moltwork rather than their own runtime.

